@* The AUTHENTICATED half of the router's NotAuthorized branch.

   Somebody is signed in and the page still said no: an ordinary user on a page their account cannot
   reach, or — the case this exists for — a kiosk tablet bounced off one of
   KioskRestrictions.RefusedPageTypes. Redirecting either of them to /account/login would be wrong
   and, for the tablet, circular: it is already signed in, so the login page hands it straight back
   to the page that refused it. This is the terminal state instead. *@

<PageTitle>Not available - Blocwerk</PageTitle>

<div class="not-authorized-notice">
    <h1>You can't open this page</h1>
    <p>
        Your account doesn't have access to it. If you're at a wall-mounted tablet, this is one of the
        pages a kiosk device is never allowed to reach — open it on your own phone or computer
        instead.
    </p>
    <a href="/" class="btn-submit">Back to Blocwerk</a>
</div>
